REM PM-DOS CONFIG.SYS
REM
REM Everything a real DOS understands is recognised here and does
REM nothing, so a CONFIG.SYS carried over from a real install boots
REM without complaint.  A release build should print NOTHING for
REM this whole file - that silence is the test.  (What a boot does
REM say is what the machine HAS: a line for a mouse and one for each
REM CD-ROM drive with its letter, and neither on a machine without.)

BUFFERS=30,8
FILES=40
FCBS=4,0
LASTDRIVE=E
STACKS=9,256
DOS=HIGH,UMB
SWITCHES=/F
NUMLOCK=OFF
EMS=AUTO
REM EMS= is PM-DOS's own statement and not one MS-DOS ever had: there
REM the manager is a driver and you load it with DEVICE=EMM386.EXE,
REM where here paging and the V86 monitor have been in the kernel
REM since v0.29 and v0.30 and there is nothing to load.  It takes
REM EMS=NO, EMS=AUTO, or a segment - EMS=D000 - and a segment
REM OVERRULES the probe, which is the point of being able to name one.
REM AUTO reserves a 64K page frame out of the same upper memory
REM DOS=UMB uses, which is why the two are answered in that order and
REM why asking for both costs the UMB pool 64K.  EMSTEST is what it
REM was written against.  Take the line out and INT 67h goes back to
REM an IRET, which is what a machine with no expanded memory should
REM have on that vector.
REM
REM DOS=UMB above is the one line here with teeth.  It probes the
REM address space between the top of memory and the system BIOS,
REM puts pages under whatever nothing decodes, and lays an MCB chain
REM over the result, which is what DEVICEHIGH and LOADHIGH load into.
REM It says nothing when it works.  Take it out and everything still
REM boots - DEVICEHIGH just means DEVICE= again, silently, exactly as
REM a real DOS with no upper-memory provider behaves.
REM
REM DEVICE= and DEVICEHIGH= LOAD the driver they name, so the line
REM below is commented out: a DEVICE= line naming a driver that is
REM not there prints a message, which would break the silence above.
REM TESTDEV.SYS is on the disk and is what the loader was written
REM against - uncomment it to watch the driver install, then TYPE
REM TESTDEV.  With DOS=UMB present it lands in upper memory and MEM
REM /D will show it there.  DEVCRIT and I24TEST need it too and cannot
REM run without it: TESTDEV refuses a '!' every other time, and it is
REM the only failure in this machine that happens on every machine.
REM DEVICEHIGH=\TESTS\TESTDEV.SYS
REM
REM And the BLOCK half of DEVICE=, which is a different job: a unit
REM is a DRIVE LETTER rather than a name.  TESTBLK.SYS is a 32K RAM
REM disk that formats itself at INIT, so uncommenting the line below
REM adds a drive letter with a filesystem already on it - the DRIVES
REM diagnostic in \TESTS shows it as "driver", and it is the letter
REM after the last real volume.  Commented out for the same reason as
REM the others: it prints a banner.
REM DEVICE=\TESTS\TESTBLK.SYS
SET PMDOSTEST=hello from config
SET TEMP=C:\TEMP
REM SET= is the master environment, which is the kernel's and which
REM the interpreter inherits: PATH and TEMP set before AUTOEXEC.BAT
REM runs, and set for every program in the machine.  These two are
REM what CFGTEST looks for.
REM
REM INSTALL= runs a program before the shell exists - what it is for
REM is TSRs - and INSTALLHIGH= puts one in upper memory.  The line
REM below is commented out because TSRTEST prints a banner and that
REM would break the silence above; uncomment it to watch a TSR load
REM before there is an interpreter, and CFGTEST's fourth check will
REM find it still answering.
REM INSTALLHIGH=\TESTS\TSRTEST.COM

REM Multi-config blocks are skipped rather than rejected.
[menu]
MENUITEM=NORMAL,Normal boot
MENUDEFAULT=NORMAL,5
[NORMAL]
INCLUDE=BASE

REM PM-DOS's own.  LFN is acted on at once: OFF makes the 71xxh API
REM answer "unsupported" and the classic calls match strict 8.3, the
REM escape hatch for a program that cannot cope with long names.  ON
REM is the default, so this line only documents it.
LFN=ON

REM DPMI=ON|OFF is the protected-mode interface.  ON is the default;
REM OFF makes INT 2Fh AX=1687h answer "no host", so a program that can
REM run either way falls back to real mode instead of failing.  This
REM line documents the default the same way LFN= above does.
DPMI=ON

REM NULLTRAP=ON|OFF is the null-pointer guard: four debug registers
REM watching the first sixteen bytes of memory for writes, so that a
REM 32-bit program dereferencing a null pointer stops instead of
REM quietly rewriting interrupt vectors 0 to 3.  ON is the default.
REM OFF is for the program that writes low memory on purpose.
NULLTRAP=ON

REM KEYRATE=rate[,delay] - how fast a held key repeats.  The numbers
REM are MODE CON's: RATE runs 1 to 32 and counts UPWARDS to faster
REM (32 is thirty characters a second), DELAY is 1 to 4 quarter-
REM seconds before it starts.  It is a CONFIG.SYS statement because
REM MODE is a program PM-DOS does not have, and it belongs with LFN=,
REM DPMI= and NULLTRAP= as a machine-wide setting the kernel owns.
REM Absent, the BIOS's setting stands - which is the right default,
REM because a statement nobody wrote should change nothing.
REM
REM AND ONE EMULATOR CANNOT TAKE THE FASTEST SETTING.  RATE=32 with
REM DELAY=1 is the byte 00h, which is a perfectly legal argument to
REM the keyboard's F3h command and means "30 a second, 250ms".  DOSBox
REM 0.74 does not implement F3h's argument state, reads that 00h as a
REM COMMAND, and stops taking keystrokes - the machine boots and then
REM will not listen.  Every other value is fine there, and so is 00h
REM on 86Box and on real hardware.  KEYRATE=31,1 is as fast as makes
REM any difference and is the one to use if a DOSBox is in the mix.
REM KEYRATE=31,1

REM IDEDMA=OFF|AUTO|ON|<mode> - how the IDE disks move their data, and
REM IDE32=OFF|AUTO|ON - whether the data port is read a doubleword at
REM a time.  They default to AUTO, as IDECABLE= does, and the lines
REM below only document that, which is why they are commented out.
REM
REM AUTO for DMA means "use the transfer mode the drive already has
REM selected, and if it has none, stay on programmed I/O".  That is
REM more cautious than it looks and the caution is the point: a
REM transfer mode is TWO settings that have to agree, the drive's and
REM the controller's timing, and only the drive's half is standard.
REM The BIOS set both at POST and made them match.  IDEDMA=ON says
REM "pick the best mode and select it".  On an Intel (PIIX, ICH) or a
REM VIA host PM-DOS sets the controller's timing to match.  On any
REM other it goes no faster than the BIOS did, or UDMA2.  IDEDMA=UDMA5
REM (UDMA0 to UDMA6) names a ceiling: that mode, or the nearest under
REM it that the drive, the host and the cable carry.  MWDMA0, MWDMA1
REM and MWDMA2 name one exactly.
REM
REM UDMA3 AND UP - 44, 66, 100 AND 133 MB/s - NEED THE 80-CONDUCTOR
REM CABLE.  Without it the mode is UDMA2, whatever IDEDMA= says.
REM IDECABLE=AUTO|40|80 is how the cable is known.  AUTO asks the
REM BIOS's choice of mode, the host adapter and the drives, in that
REM order.  40 and 80 are for a machine where AUTO gets it wrong, and
REM apply to both channels.  Write IDECABLE= above IDEDMA=: each line
REM takes effect as it is read.
REM
REM A failed bus-master transfer takes that disk off DMA for the rest
REM of the session and repeats the transfer by PIO, so a machine whose
REM chipset misbehaves gets a slow disk rather than a wrong one.  A
REM CRC error in a mode past UDMA2 brings that disk down to UDMA2
REM instead, and at UDMA2 or under takes it off DMA.  Set IDEDMA=OFF
REM to have programmed I/O from the start.
REM
REM IDE32=OFF is the one to reach for if a machine reads rubbish off a
REM disk that a real DOS reads perfectly: an adapter that cannot latch
REM two words does not report an error, it returns half the data.
REM AUTO tries it before using it - the drive's IDENTIFY block is read
REM both ways, and the wide way is used only if the two agree - and
REM IDETEST says when it was tried and refused.  IDE32=ON does not try.
REM
REM IDETEST.EXE reports what was chosen and checks that the same
REM sectors read through the wide path and the narrow one agree.
REM IDEDMA=AUTO
REM IDE32=AUTO
REM IDECABLE=AUTO
REM
REM CDROM= puts the first CD-ROM on a letter of your choosing; the
REM rest follow it.  Without it they land after the hard disks.
REM CDROM=M

REM CACHE=max[,min[,ON|OFF]] - the disk cache, and it is on by default
REM with no line here at all.  Sizes are in KILOBYTES, which is what
REM SMARTDRV's command line took; a K or an M suffix says so, so
REM CACHE=4M and CACHE=4096 are the same line.  The third field is
REM lazy writing.
REM
REM THE TWO NUMBERS ARE A RANGE AND NOT A SIZE, and that is the whole
REM design of it.  The cache lives between them: it grows on a miss
REM while the arena has memory to spare, and it hands memory BACK when
REM a program asks for more than is left, flushing what it owes on the
REM way out.  Naming one number still gives a range, because a cache
REM that cannot shrink is memory a program can never have.  To pin it,
REM name the same number twice - CACHE=2048,2048.
REM
REM Absent, both bounds come from the size of extended memory: an
REM eighth of the arena for the ceiling, a quarter of that for the
REM floor, and NO CACHE AT ALL on a machine with less than two
REM megabytes of arena to spend, because there the memory is wanted
REM for programs.
REM
REM WHAT LAZY WRITING PROMISES.  With it on - the default, and only
REM ever for FIXED DISKS - a write to a hard disk is held in memory
REM and the several writes that DOS makes to one FAT sector during a
REM copy become one transfer.  Everything owed is written before the
REM command prompt comes back, because COMMAND.COM calls INT 21h
REM AH=0Dh there and the kernel treats it as the commit point; a
REM program ending commits too.  So the window in which switching the
REM machine off loses a write is one command long and never includes
REM the time you spend thinking about the next one.  CACHE=...,OFF
REM closes even that window, at the cost of the coalescing.
REM
REM FLOPPIES, CD-ROMS AND LOADED BLOCK DRIVERS ARE READ-CACHED ONLY
REM and there is no switch for it.  A diskette can be swapped between
REM two commands with nothing to tell us; an ATAPI drive reports a
REM disc change only as a failure on the next command it is given; a
REM block driver is somebody else's code.  Holding a write for any of
REM those means holding data for a medium that may not be there.
REM Their cached blocks are dropped at every commit as well, so a
REM stale read cannot outlive one command either.
REM
REM VERIFY defeats lazy writing while it is on, because 21h/2Eh asks
REM for a write to be read back and there is nothing to read back
REM until the sector has been written.
REM
REM THE CACHE ANSWERS SMARTDRV'S INTERFACE, INT 2Fh AX=4A10h, so
REM SMARTDRV.EXE and anything like it finds a cache already installed
REM and does not load a second one on top - two caches holding two
REM versions of one sector being the thing all of this exists to
REM avoid.  \TESTS\CACHETST asks it the ten questions SMARTMON asks,
REM from inside a DOS box, which is the only place that API lives.
REM
REM CACHE /S AT THE PROMPT IS SMARTDRV /S: the hits and the misses,
REM the three sizes, and which letters are read- and write-cached.
REM CACHE /C writes what is owed, CACHE /R empties it, and CACHE C+ A-
REM sets write-behind per letter, as SMARTDRV's did.
REM
REM CACHE=OFF turns the whole thing off and puts the machine back to
REM how it behaved before v0.59.
REM CACHE=2048,512,ON

REM SHARE=locks[,space] | ON | OFF - file sharing and record locking,
REM which was SHARE.EXE and is in the kernel since v0.60.  ON with
REM twenty locks is the default, so the line below only documents it.
REM The number is SHARE.EXE's /L: - how many byte ranges may be locked
REM at once, up to 1024.  A second number is its /F:, the space it
REM kept file names in; it is accepted so that a line carried over
REM from a real DOS is not an error, and means nothing here - a file
REM is known by where its directory entry is, and no names are kept.
REM
REM WHAT IT DOES is what SHARE.EXE did, by SHARE.EXE's own tables: an
REM open in a deny mode is refused when the file is already open in a
REM way it would deny, and a compatibility-mode open is refused when
REM the file is open in any deny mode ("Sharing violation", through
REM INT 24h, error 20h if the handler fails it); 5Ch locks a byte
REM range and refuses one that overlaps another lock on the same file
REM (error 21h); and a read or a write into a range some OTHER handle
REM has locked is refused ("Lock violation", INT 24h, error 21h).
REM Locks go when the handle closes or the program ends.  INT 2Fh
REM AX=1000h says installed, so SHARE.EXE itself declines to load.
REM
REM SHARE=OFF is a DOS with no SHARE.EXE: every open succeeds, every
REM lock is granted, and 2Fh/1000h is unanswered.  \TESTS\SHARETST
REM asks sixteen questions and expects SHARE.EXE's answers to each.
REM SHARE=20

REM UNDELETE=ON | OFF - delete protection, in the kernel since v0.60.
REM ON is the default, so the line below only documents it.  A deleted
REM file is not freed: it is moved into a hidden RECYCLED directory on
REM its own drive (made on the first delete there), and UNDELETE.EXE
REM lists it, puts it back, or clears the lot out for good (/PURGE).
REM It works for every program - 32-bit ones, 16-bit ones in a DOS box,
REM DEL through the FCB calls as well as the handle ones - except under
REM Windows 3.1 in 386 enhanced mode, whose DOS is RMDOS.SYS: what is
REM deleted there is deleted as before.
REM
REM Kept files may use up to 10% of the space that would be free
REM without them; the oldest go first when a new one needs the room,
REM and a file bigger than that on its own is deleted as before.  So
REM are *.TMP and *.$$$ files (the shells' pipes are %PIPEn.$$$), and
REM anything deleted from inside RECYCLED itself.  MOVE, XCOPY and
REM COMMAND.COM's COPY delete a copy they have just made safe
REM elsewhere, or could not finish, without keeping it.
REM
REM UNDELETE=OFF deletes as DOS always has.
REM UNDELETE=ON
